# .forceignore — what this project does NOT track.
#
# Copy to the project root as `.forceignore` and COMMIT it. Like CLAUDE.md and
# the hooks, an ignored .forceignore does not exist in any worktree, and a
# worktree without it will happily retrieve and deploy things this file exists
# to keep out.
#
# gitignore syntax, relative to the project root. Applies to BOTH retrieve and
# deploy: a line here means "this project does not have an opinion about this",
# in both directions.
#
# Every block below names its reason class. When you hit a type that is not
# listed, classify it rather than guessing:
#
#   NOT AUTHORED      a user made it by clicking, and will keep making more
#   NOT PORTABLE      carries secrets or org-bound ids a deploy cannot reproduce
#   NOT YOURS         belongs to an installed package
#   SUPERSEDED        the platform replaced it and retrieves it for compatibility
#   GOVERNED ELSEWHERE this skill already has a mechanism for it
#
# Lines marked JUDGMENT are ones the project has to decide. Decide them during
# seeding, not later. Delete the ones you disagree with and say why in CLAUDE.md.
#
# See references/seeding-guide.md for the reasoning behind each block.


# ── GOVERNED ELSEWHERE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Permissions live in ONE generated permission set, derived from the source tree
# by scripts/gen-permset.py. A retrieved profile is a second, hand-edited answer
# to the same question: it conflicts on every parallel build and carries FLS for
# fields that may not exist in the branch being merged into. Salesforce is also
# removing most profile-level permissions in Spring '26.
**/profiles/**
**/profilePasswordPolicies/**
**/profileSessionSettings/**

# Permission set ASSIGNMENTS are per-user org state, not configuration.
**/permissionsetassignments/**


# ── NOT PORTABLE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Connected Apps and External Client Apps do not retrieve their consumer key or
# secret, and their OAuth policy blocks are org-scoped. What comes down deploys
# without error and produces an integration that cannot authenticate — a silent
# failure. Create these in Setup; document their names in CLAUDE.md.
**/connectedApps/**
**/externalClientApps/**
**/extlClntAppConfigurablePolicies/**
**/extlClntAppGlobalOauthSettings/**
**/extlClntAppMobileConfigurablePolicies/**
**/extlClntAppMobileSettings/**
**/extlClntAppNotificationSettings/**
**/extlClntAppOauthConfigurablePolicies/**
**/extlClntAppOauthSettings/**
**/extlClntAppPushConfigurablePolicies/**
**/extlClntAppPushSettings/**
**/extlClntAppSamlConfigurablePolicies/**

# Certificates retrieve without their private key. SSO config is org-bound.
**/certs/**
**/certificates/**
**/samlssoconfigs/**

# Territory and forecasting hierarchies are bound to org user ids.
**/territory2Models/**


# ── NOT AUTHORED ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Users create these by clicking, and keep creating more. Tracking them turns
# every user's Tuesday into a diff and every seed into a merge conflict.
**/reports/**
**/dashboards/**
**/documents/**
**/analyticSnapshots/**

# Custom report types are authored metadata and stay tracked. Reports and
# dashboards above are excluded by default because their folder contents are
# user-curated. Replace the broad exclusions only after the project names the
# folders it deliberately owns.

# JUDGMENT — email templates.
# Exclude if marketing or admins author them in Setup (the common case).
# Delete these two lines if templates are authored in the repo and reviewed
# in PRs, which is the better practice where the team will actually do it.
**/email/**
**/emailTemplates/**

# JUDGMENT — list views.
# List views scoped to "Mine" cannot be deployed at all; the platform rejects
# them. If your org has any, either exclude list views wholesale (uncomment
# below) or exclude the specific offenders by path. Leaving them in means every
# whole-tree dry run fails on something you were never going to fix.
# **/listViews/**


# ── NOT YOURS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Installed package metadata. `sf project generate manifest --from-org` already
# excludes managed and unlocked packages by default, so this is a backstop for
# retrieves that name types directly. Namespaced components are `ns__Thing__c`:
# two underscore pairs. `__` is reserved by the platform, so the pattern cannot
# false-positive on a name you chose.
**/installedPackages/**
**/*__*__c.field-meta.xml
**/*__*__c.object-meta.xml


# ── SUPERSEDED ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# FlowDefinition was replaced by Flow's own activeVersionNumber. It still
# retrieves, and it still causes deploy conflicts with the flows it shadows.
**/flowDefinitions/**

# Data.com is retired. AppMenu is org-arrangement state, not configuration.
**/cleanDataServices/**
**/appMenus/**


# ── STANDARD OBJECT NOISE ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Retrieving a standard object brings every standard field with it. Those are
# platform-owned and never change under you.
#
# There is no pattern that says "standard fields only", because standard field
# names have no marker — `Name`, `Industry`, `Phone`. The seeding guide handles
# this with a find-and-review pass (Step 5) instead, which is why this block is
# guidance rather than rules.
#
# What you CAN pin here is any standard object the project has decided it does
# not own at all. One line each, so the decision is visible:
#
# force-app/main/default/objects/Individual/**
# force-app/main/default/objects/ContentVersion/**


# ── LOCAL NOISE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Never deployable, occasionally retrieved into the tree by accident.
**/jsconfig.json
**/.eslintrc.json
**/lwc/.eslintrc.json
**/aura/.eslintrc.json
**/.DS_Store
